# Theme adherence is required for all UI

Flashlearn is fully themeable at runtime: a "mode" (light/dark/system) and a
"palette" (brand + accent colors) are materialized into CSS custom properties on
`:root` by `frontend/apps/web/src/utils/themeController.js`. The catalog in
`frontend/apps/web/src/constants/themes.js` is the single source of truth.

Whenever you ADD or UPDATE any UI, it MUST use these theme tokens so it adapts to
light/dark and every palette. Verify your change looks correct in both light and
dark mode.

## Use the tokens — never hardcode brand/neutral colors

- **In SCSS** (`frontend/apps/web/src/styles/sass/**`): use the Sass aliases from
  `base/_variables.scss`, e.g. `$main-purple`, `$blue`, `$yellow`, `$surface`,
  `$surface-2`, `$app-background`, `$main-text-color`, `$minor-text-color`,
  `$gray-text-color`, `$border-subtle`, `$border-color`, `$brand-gradient`, and
  the shadow/radius/space scales. Prefer the `@mixin`s (`card`,
  `card_interactive`, `purple_btn`, `blue_btn`, `yellow_btn`).
- **In JS/MUI `sx` or inline styles**: reference the CSS variables directly, e.g.
  `color: "var(--fl-text)"`, `backgroundColor: "var(--fl-surface)"`,
  `borderColor: "var(--fl-border)"`, `background: "var(--fl-gradient)"`. For
  translucency use the `-rgb` triplets:
  `rgba(var(--fl-primary-rgb), 0.12)`.

### Token cheat-sheet (CSS var ↔ SCSS alias)

- Brand: `--fl-primary` / `$main-purple`, `--fl-primary-dark` / `$dark-purple`,
  `--fl-primary-hover` / `$hover-purple`, `--fl-on-primary` / `$on-primary`,
  `--fl-gradient` / `$brand-gradient`
- Accents: `--fl-accent` / `$blue`, `--fl-accent-2` / `$yellow` (+ `-strong`)
- Neutrals: `--fl-bg` / `$app-background`, `--fl-surface` / `$surface`,
  `--fl-surface-2` / `$surface-2`, `--fl-text` / `$main-text-color`,
  `--fl-text-minor` / `$minor-text-color`, `--fl-text-muted` /
  `$gray-text-color`, `--fl-border` / `$border-subtle`, `--fl-border-strong` /
  `$border-color`

## Don'ts

- Don't hardcode hex/`rgb()` for brand, accent, surface, text, or border colors
  (e.g. `#fff`, `#282e3e`, `#4255ff`). Use a token instead.
- Don't assume a white background or dark text — both flip in dark mode.
- Only static *semantic* colors may stay hardcoded: success green, error red
  (`$error-red`), and similar status colors that must not change with the theme.
